    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` field per the specification: it is `true` when **any** of the three signals hold, otherwise `false`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `riskFlag` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the OpenAPI generator produces `OwnerDto.setRiskFlag(...)`.\n\n2. **`RejectDisposableEmailDomain.java`** \u2014 Extracted `domainOf(email)` and added `adjacent(email)`. \"Disposable-adjacent\" is defined as a **subdomain of a known disposable domain** (e.g. `mail.mailinator.com`). This is the meaningful softer case, since an *exact* disposable domain is already rejected with 400 at create time and never reaches a stored owner.\n\n3. **`CityCapacity.java`** \u2014 Added `overSoftCapacity(...)`: true when the city holds at least the soft threshold (40) owners \u2014 unlike the existing `warning` band (40\u201349), this stays true at and above the hard limit too, matching \"over its soft capacity\".\n\n4. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new) \u2014 Combines the three signals: possible duplicate (`owner.getPossibleDuplicate()`), disposable-adjacent email, or city over soft capacity.\n\n5. **`RespondWithOwner.java` / `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 Set `dto.setRiskFlag(RiskFlag.of(owner, ownerRepository))`, following the existing `capacityWarning` pattern (these are the only spots with both the entity and the repository).\n\nThe test `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` \u2014 a fully-unique structured owner (no duplicate, no email, low city count) \u2014 correctly reads back `riskFlag: false`, and the whole suite passes.",
